From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 17:16:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] SecureElement: start the process when an application binds
 the service

SecureElement is the OMAPI service for an embedded secure element or a
UICC reader. The application is persistent, so the system starts its
process at every boot and SEApplication.onCreate then starts the
service; the process holds about 5.7 MiB at idle whether or not an
SEService client ever asks for it. A device without a reader has no use
for that: its clients find no reader, but they still have to connect.

SEService looks for the system's secure_element service first and, when
that is missing, binds the application's SecureElementService intent.
Remove android:persistent, so nothing starts the process at boot and the
first such bind does.

The bind creates the service. SecureElementService.onCreate registers
secure_element, and the vendor-stable entry where the product enables
it, and onBind returns the OMAPI binder, so nothing needs the service to
be started as well. Remove the startService call from
SEApplication.onCreate. The system starts the process of a bound
service in the background, and a background application may not call
startService: the application crashed with
BackgroundServiceStartNotAllowedException in onCreate on every bind and
no client connected. A persistent application is exempt, which is why
the call worked before.

The service is bound, not started. It lives while a client is bound and
is destroyed with the last unbind, which closes its terminals; the
secure_element entry stays until the process dies, and the next bind
creates the service again. A device without a reader has no terminals.
It is not sticky either: the system does not restart it after reclaiming
the process, so the process is one that it may reclaim under memory
pressure and leave reclaimed until the next bind.
---
 AndroidManifest.xml                   |  1 -
 src/com/android/se/SEApplication.java | 13 +++++--------
 2 files changed, 5 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/AndroidManifest.xml b/AndroidManifest.xml
index 9e3a75bb8b90610cb94392f82eece45045106c10..d2034975b60f8059f7cc057d6ac26673dfee42f0 100755
--- a/AndroidManifest.xml
+++ b/AndroidManifest.xml
@@ -7,7 +7,6 @@
     <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES"/>
     <application android:name=".SEApplication"
          android:label="SecureElementApplication"
-         android:persistent="true"
          android:directBootAware="true"
          android:defaultToDeviceProtectedStorage="true"
          android:memtagMode="async">
diff --git a/src/com/android/se/SEApplication.java b/src/com/android/se/SEApplication.java
index 55a9e84b44fc42ed46e2aaafafc25fd7cca30838..ac407c264e75e3a4056a6ace45a5a8da8fbb8e32 100644
--- a/src/com/android/se/SEApplication.java
+++ b/src/com/android/se/SEApplication.java
@@ -17,16 +17,13 @@
 package com.android.se;
 
 import android.app.Application;
-import android.content.Intent;
 
 /**
- * Starts the SecureElementService.
+ * The SecureElement application. It is not persistent and starts nothing: the process starts when
+ * an SEService client binds SecureElementService, and that bind creates the service, whose
+ * onCreate registers it. The application must not start the service itself: the system starts the
+ * process of a bound service in the background and refuses a background startService, which
+ * crashed the application on every bind.
  */
 public class SEApplication extends Application {
-    @Override
-    public void onCreate() {
-        super.onCreate();
-        Intent serviceIntent = new Intent(getApplicationContext(), SecureElementService.class);
-        startService(serviceIntent);
-    }
 }
